Write at least **150 words**.","image_url":"https://media.ieltsai.pro/writing/c16-t1/task-1.jpg","sample_answer":"<h2 class="ql-align-center">Changes in Appliance Ownership and Housework Time (1920 - 2019)</h2><p>The provided set of charts details two aspects of household life in a specific country from 1920 to 2019: the ownership of electrical appliances and the time dedicated to housework.</p><p>In the chart about appliance ownership, in 1920, the percentage of households with refrigerators was close to zero, while for washing machines and vacuum cleaners, it was around 40% and 30% respectively. By 1940, the proportion of households with refrigerators increased significantly to around 55%, slightly overtaking the ownership rate of vacuum cleaners, but still remained below the 60% rate of washing machines.</p><p>The ownership of vacuum cleaners grew steadily over the years, reaching a plateau close to 100% around 2000 and maintaining that level through 2019. Refrigerator ownership reached 100% even earlier in 1980 and remained at that level through 2019. The percentage of households with washing machines increased gradually from 1920 to 1960, approaching 70%, before briefly dropping to approximately 62% in 1980. It then continued to slowly increase again until reaching about 75% in 2019.</p><p>Turning to the housework-related chart, in 1920, an average household spent approximately 50 hours per week on housework, which included washing clothes, preparing meals, and cleaning. This number declined continuously over the years. By 1960, it had dropped to around 20 hours, and by 2019, it further decreased to roughly 11 hours per week.</p><p>In summary, the increased prevalence of electrical appliances in households seems to have coincided with a substantial reduction in the time spent on housework over the 99-year period.</p>"},"Task 2":{"prompt":"<p><strong><em>In some countries, more and more people are becoming interested in finding out about the history of the house or building they live in.</em></strong></p><p><strong><em>What are the reasons for this?</em></strong></p><p><strong><em>How can people research this?</em></strong></p>","instructions":"You should spend about **40 minutes** on this task.

Write at least **250 words**.","image_url":"","sample_answer":"<h2 class="ql-align-center">Reasons and Research Methods for People's Interest in the History of Their Living Places</h2><p>The growing curiosity about residential history stems from our innate desire to connect with the past through tangible surroundings. This trend reflects both personal identity formation and collective cultural preservation efforts, enabled by modern accessibility of historical data.</p><p>One primary driver is the increasing value placed on heritage conservation. Homeowners often discover that understanding a building's past helps preserve architectural integrity during renovations. For instance, those residing in Amsterdam's canal houses frequently uncover original 17th-century features beneath modern layers, prompting careful restoration rather than demolition. Additionally, historical awareness satisfies emotional needs - knowing a Victorian-era London terrace survived wartime bombings creates deeper emotional attachment to living spaces.</p><p>The digital revolution has democratized historical research through various channels. Local council archives now digitize property records, enabling online access to blueprints and ownership history. The UK's "Historic England" database allows postcode-based searches of listed buildings with construction details. Physical investigation also yields clues - examining attic beams in New England colonial homes might reveal shipbuilding techniques through tool marks and joint types. Oral histories remain valuable; Chicago residents often trace brownstone histories through elderly neighbors' recollections of previous occupants.</p><p>Community initiatives further facilitate discovery. Toronto's "House History Project" trains volunteers to interpret census records and fire insurance maps, while Melbourne's heritage walks emphasize architectural storytelling. These approaches transform private curiosity into collective knowledge preservation. Ultimately, researching a building's past satisfies both practical preservation needs and the human yearning for connection across generations, turning houses into living history books rather than mere shelters.</p>"}}

{"Task 1":{"prompt":"<p><strong><em>The diagram below shows how electricity is generated in a hydroelectric power station.</em></strong></p><p><strong><em>Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.</em></strong></p>","instructions":"You should spend about **20 minutes** on this task.

Write at least **150 words**.","image_url":"https://media.ieltsai.pro/writing/c14-t3/task-1.jpg","sample_answer":"<h2 class="ql-align-center">Process of Electricity Generation in a Hydroelectric Power Station</h2><p>The diagram illustrates the process of electricity generation in a hydroelectric power station.</p><p>Water from the river is stored in a high-level reservoir behind a dam. During the day, the intake is open, and water flows down through a channel to the power station. As the water passes through reversible turbines, it drives the generator to produce electricity, which is then transmitted to the national grid via power lines. The used water then enters a low - level reservoir.</p><p>At night, when electricity demand is lower, the intake is closed. The reversible turbines switch function and act as pumps. They take water from the low-level reservoir and pump it back up to the high-level reservoir, ready for the next day's electricity generation.</p><p>This system effectively utilizes the gravitational potential energy of water to generate electricity, and the reversible turbines play a crucial role in both electricity production during the day and water recycling at night, making it a sustainable and efficient power-generation method.</p><p class="ql-align-center"><br></p>"},"Task 2":{"prompt":"<p><strong><em>Some people say that music is a good way of bringing people of different cultures and ages together.</em></strong></p><p><strong><em>To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?</em></strong></p>","instructions":"You should spend about **40 minutes** on this task.

Write at least **250 words**.","image_url":"","sample_answer":"<h2 class="ql-align-center">Is Music a Good Way to Unite People of Different Cultures and Ages?</h2><p>The idea that music bridges cultural and generational divides is a powerful one, and I wholeheartedly agree with its transformative potential. While challenges like cultural preferences or generational tastes exist, music's unique ability to evoke emotions and create shared experiences far outweighs these barriers.</p><p>One compelling example of music's unifying power is its role in global events. The Olympic Games, for instance, often feature anthems that blend diverse cultural elements. When artists from different countries collaborate on a theme song, such as "We Are the World" for famine relief or "Waka Waka" for the FIFA World Cup, these melodies transcend language and geography. People across continents sing along, not just because of the tune, but because the music conveys universal messages of hope, unity, and celebration.</p><p>Music also fosters intergenerational bonds. Families, for example, frequently bond over shared playlists during gatherings. A grandmother might introduce her grandchildren to jazz standards, while the younger generation shares modern pop or hip-hop. This exchange creates mutual respect and understanding. Similarly, iconic artists like The Beatles or Bob Dylan continue to resonate with new generations, proving that timeless melodies can erase age gaps. Conversely, collaborations between established and emerging artists, such as Elton John and Ed Sheeran's "Merry Christmas", attract listeners of all ages, merging classic and contemporary styles.</p><p>Critics might argue that cultural or generational differences limit music's reach. For instance, traditional Japanese gagaku or Peruvian panpipe music may not appeal to everyone. However, even these genres find global appreciation through platforms like streaming services or international festivals, where curiosity and exposure override initial unfamiliarity.</p><p>In conclusion, music serves as a universal language that unites people across cultures and generations. Whether through global events, family interactions, or digital platforms, its capacity to evoke emotions and build connections remains unparalleled. While diversity in tastes exists, music's inherent ability to create shared meaning ensures it remains a powerful force for harmony in an increasingly divided world.</p>"}}

